**Subject:** Playwright framework training for ENTS and BISS QA: 8 weekly sessions starting [start date]

Hi all,

Now that the Playwright framework is running nightly on Jenkins, we would like everyone in the ENTS and BISS QA teams to be comfortable running it and reading its results. To get there, we have put together a short training plan and I would like to share it with you.

**The format**

- One 30-minute session a week for eight weeks, on [day and time].
- Each session covers one topic: a 10-minute live demo, then 10 minutes of hands-on practice.
- For the hands-on part we split into two groups, so ENTS testers practise on ENTS test cases and BISS testers on BISS test cases.
- There is a small task of about 20 minutes between sessions.
- No coding is needed. Every session is recorded for anyone who misses a week.

**The goal**

By the end of week 8, each of us can start a run, read the reports and triage a failed test on our own. That means saying whether a failure is an application defect, a test data problem or a locator issue, and raising it with the right evidence.

**The schedule**

| Week | Session | What we will cover | What you will be able to do |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | The big picture | One live run, and how a test travels from a commit to the dashboard | Describe how the framework runs a test |
| 2 | Starting a run | Choosing test cases in the execution sheet and running them from IntelliJ | Run a test case on your own laptop |
| 3 | Reading the results | The step log, the Word report, the Test Dashboard and the stakeholder PDF | Say what passed, what failed and where |
| 4 | Triage | Failure causes, screenshots and replaying a failed step | Sort a failure into defect, data or locator |
| 5 | How a test case reads | A feature file, step by step, in business language | Read a test case and say what it covers |
| 6 | Where test data comes from | How herds and logins are taken from the database before each run | Tell a data problem from a defect |
| 7 | Locators and self-healing | The Object Repository, and what the framework fixes by itself | Report a locator issue with the right details |
| 8 | Jenkins, and sign-off | The nightly job, starting a build and finding its reports | Start a build and triage its result unaided |

Weeks 1 to 4 are a walkthrough of how the framework executes. Weeks 5 to 8 cover the technical side, kept to what a tester needs when a test fails.

**What we need before week 1**

- IntelliJ and the project set up on your laptop. We will help with this.
- Access to the GitLab project and the Jenkins job.
- A working test login on SIT or CENTEST.

**What this gives the team**

Today the nightly results are read by one person. After the training, the tester who knows the application best can triage a failure the same morning, and we plan to rotate that between one ENTS and one BISS tester each week.

Please let me know by [reply-by date] if the proposed slot does not suit you, or if you need help with access. The full plan, with the weekly tasks and the sign-off checklist, is attached.

Thanks,
Aniket

